task: bench-java-stream-lane
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/bench-java-stream-lane
worktree: ../okay-wt-java-lane
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §20 (the lane list)

why: the operator's ask, 2026-09-10 — "давай ещё сделаем тот же
  бенчмарк на java stream через наш интероп". It is the same argument
  §20 already rests on, one engine further: okay-java's
  `Collect.collector` says an okay `Aggregator` IS a JDK `Collector`
  (supplier/accumulator/combiner/finisher against init/add/merge/
  present), exactly as `FlinkInterop.toFlink` says it is an
  `AggregateFunction`. So `Job.stats` — the one value the okay and
  Flink lanes share — can run on a third engine, and the third engine
  is the one every JVM already has.

what: `JavaLane`, the same five stages over `java.util.stream`,
  sequential and parallel, sourced through `Streams.stream(chunks,
  parallel)` (okay-java's spliterator, which splits per chunk and
  claims ORDERED — which is what keeps the bunching stage's per-key
  arrival order, and so the same answer). It must produce the SAME
  eleven checksums as the okay and Flink lanes; the suite asserts that
  before timing. okay-java joins okay-flink's TEST classpath only.

predict: parallel() beats the Flink lane on this box and loses to one
  okay thread or comes close; the interesting number is not the
  ratio but what the model costs — a JDK stream is single-use (three
  consumers become THREE PASSES) and has no event time at all, so a
  "window" is only a key and every pane of the whole run stays live.
  That is the row §20 needs: not "slower", but "no watermark, so the
  state is the history".
